Scott 1976, Theorem 1.3, the binary diagonal. If a map of two arguments is Scott continuous in each argument separately, then its value on the diagonal at x equals the union over n of the codes k for which the finite set of n is contained in x and k lies in the map at that finite set in both arguments.

If a code lies on the diagonal, continuity in the first argument writes it as a member of the Scott union in that argument and supplies a finite witness, and continuity in the second argument at that witness supplies a second finite witness. The bitwise join of the two codes has finite set containing both witnesses, and monotonicity in each argument places the code in the diagonal value at that join. Conversely, a code witnessed by a finite set contained in x is placed on the diagonal by two applications of monotonicity.
